"hadiths[303]{hadithnumber,text}:"
"149","the Prophet said: ""Jibril (peace be upon him) led me (in Salat) twice at the House. So he prayed Zuhr the first time when the shadow was similar to (the length of) the strap a sandal. Then he prayed Asr when everything was similar (to the length of) its shadow. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun had set and the fasting person breaks fast. Then he prayed Isha when the twilight had vanished. Then he prayed Fajr when Fajr (dawn) began, and when eating is prohibited for the fasting person. The second time he prayed Zuhr when the shadow of everything was similar to (the length of) it, at the time of Asr the day before. Then he prayed Asr when the shadow of everything was about twice as long as it. Then he prayed Maghrib at the same time as he did the first time. Then he prayed Isha, the later one, when a third of the night had gone. Then he prayed Subh when the land glowed. Then Jibril turned towards me and said: ""O Muhammad! These are the times of the Prophets before you, and the (best) time is what is between these two times."""
150,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Indeed for (the time of) Salat (there is a) beginning and an end. The beginning of the time for the Zuhr prayer is when the sun passes the zenith"
"151","Allah's Messenger said: ""Indeed for (the time of) Salat (there is a) beginning and an end. The beginning of the time for the Zuhr prayer is when the sun passes the zenith, and the end of its time is when the time for Asr enters. The beginning of the time for the Asr  is when its time enters, and the end of its time is when the sun yellows (turns pale). The beginning of the time of Maghrib is when the sun as set, and the end of its time is when the twilight has vanished (i.e., the horizon is invisible because of darkness). The beginning of the time for Isha, the later one, is when the horizon has vanished, and the end of its time is when the night is at its half. The beginning of the time for Fajr is when Fajr begins, and its end is when the sun rises."""
"152","""A man came to the Prophet to ask him about the times for Salat. So he said: 'Stay with us, In sha Allah.' So he ordered Bilal to call the Iqamah when Fajr began, then he ordered him to call the Iqamah when the Sun passed the zenith, then he prayed Zuhr. Then he ordered him to call the Iqamah to pray Asr while the sun was elevated and white. Then he ordered him (to call the Iqamah for) Maghrib when the (top) edge of the sun had set. Then he ordered him to call the Iqamah for Isha when the horizon (twilight) had vanished. Then he ordered him in the morning (to give the call for Fajr prayer), when the light of Fajr glowed. Then he ordered (him to call the Iqamah for) Zuhr, so he waited well until it had cooled. Then he ordered (him to call the Iqamah for) Asr, so he calIed the Iqamah while the sun was later in its position than what it was (the day before). Then he ordered him to delay Maghrib until right before the twilight had disappeared. Then he ordered (him to call the Iqamah for) Isha, so he called the Iqamah when a third of the night had passed. Then he said: 'Where is the one who asked about he times for the Salat?' So the man said, 'It is I.' So he said: 'The times [or the Salat are what are between these two.'"""
"153","""Allah's Messenger would pray Subh (at such time that) the women would leave (after the prayer)"" - AI-AnsarI (one of the narrators) said - the women would pass by wrapped in their Mirts and they would not be recognizable due to the darkness."" And Qutaibah said: ""covered."" (instead of ""wrapped."")"
154,"Aishah narrated:""I have not seen anyone who hastened Zuhr more than Allah's Messenger nor Abu Bakr"
155,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""Allah's Messenger prayed Zuhr when the sun had passed the zenith"
156,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""In very hot weather"
"157","Allah's Messenger said: ""In very hot weather, delay the (Zuhr) prayer until it becomes (a bit) cooler, because the severity of heat is from the raging of the Hell."""
"158","""Allah's Messenger was on a journey and Bilal was with him. So he wanted to call for the prayer, but he (the Prophet) said: 'Let it get cooler.' Then he wanted to call for the prayer, so Allah's Messenger said: 'Let it get to the cooler time of Zuhr.'"" He (i.e., Abu Dharr) said: ""Until we saw the shadows of the hillocks, then he commanded that the Iqamah be called and then led the people in prayer. Allah's Messenger said: 'The severity of heat is from the raging of Hell, so wait until it becomes cooler for the (Zuhr) prayer.'"""
159,"Al-Ala bin Abdur-Raman narrated that :he visited Anas bin Malik in his home in Al Basrah after finishing Zuhr"
"160","he visited Anas bin Malik in his home in Al Basrah after finishing Zuhr, and his home was next to the Masjid. So he said: 'Stand to pray Asr."" He (Al-Ala) said: ""So we stood to pray. When we were finished he (Anas) said: 'I heard Allah's Messenger saying: ""That is the prayer of the hypocrite. He sits watching the sun, until when it is between the horns of the Shaitan he stands and pecks out four (units of prayer), not remembering Allah in them but a little."""
161,"Narrator not mentioned:[And I have in my book: ""Ali bin Hujr informed me from lsma'il bin Ibrahim"
162,"Similar narration is reported by Bishr ibn Mu'adh Al-Bari who said that :Isma'il ibn Ulayyah narrated to him from Ibn Juraij. This latter chain is more correct"
163,"Salmah bin AI-Akwa narrated:""Allah's Messenger prayed Maghrib when the sun had set and it (the sun) had hidden in the veil (of darkness)"
164,"An-Nu'man bin Bashir said:""I am the most knowledgeable among the people about the prescribed time of this prayer: Allah's Messenger would pray it when the moon set on the third (of the month)"
"165","""I am the most knowledgeable among the people about the prescribed time of this prayer: Allah's Messenger would pray it when the moon set on the third (of the month)."""
166,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""If it were not that it would be a hardship on my Ummah"
"167","Allah's Messenger said: ""If it were not that it would be a hardship on my Ummah, then I would have ordered you to delay Isha until the third of the night, or its half."""
168,"Umar bin Al-Khattab narrated:""Allah's Messenger would talk during the night with Abu Bakr about matters concerning the Muslims while I was with them"
169,"Umm Farwah - and she was one of those who gave pledge of allegiance to the Prophet - narrated:""The Prophet was asked: 'Which deed is the best?' So he said: 'Salat in the beginning of its time"
170,"Ali bin AbI Talib narrated that :the Prophet said to him: 'Ali! Three are not to be delayed: Salat when its time comes"
"171","the Prophet said to him: 'Ali! Three are not to be delayed: Salat when its time comes, a funeral whet it (a prepared body) is present, and the (marriage of a) single woman when there is an equal for her."""
172,"Abu Amr Ash-Shaibani narrated:""A man said to Ibn Mas'ud: 'Which deed is most virtuous?' He said: 'I asked Allah's Messenger (that). He said: ""Salat at the beginning of its time."" I asked him: ""What is after that O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Being dutiful to one's parents."" I said: ""What is after that [O Messenger of Allah]?"" He said: ""Jihad in the Way of Allah"
"173","""A man said to Ibn Mas'ud: 'Which deed is most virtuous?' He said: 'I asked Allah's Messenger (that). He said: ""Salat at the beginning of its time."" I asked him: ""What is after that O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Being dutiful to one's parents."" I said: ""What is after that ?"" He said: ""Jihad in the Way of Allah."""
"174","""Allah's Messenger did not pray any Salat at the end of its time two times, until Allah took him."""
175,"Abu Dharr narrated that :the Prophet said: ""O Abu Dharr! There will be leaders after me who cause the Salat to die; so perform the Salat during its time. If you pray (with them) during its time"
"176","the Prophet said: ""O Abu Dharr! There will be leaders after me who cause the Salat to die; so perform the Salat during its time. If you pray (with them) during its time, then that will be voluntary Salat for you, if not, then you will have attained your Salat."""
"177","""They asked the Prophet about when they sIept past the Salat. He said: 'There is no negligence in sleep, negligence is only while one is awake. So when one of you forgets a Salat, or sleeps through it, then let him pray it when he remembers it.'"""
178,"Abdullah (bin Mas'ud] narrated:""The idolaters kept Allah's Messenger distracted from four prayers on the Day of Al-Khandaq (the battle of the Trench) until as much as Allah willed of the night had passed. So he ordered Bilal to call the Adhan"
"179","""The idolaters kept Allah's Messenger distracted from four prayers on the Day of Al-Khandaq (the battle of the Trench) until as much as Allah willed of the night had passed. So he ordered Bilal to call the Adhan, then he called the Iqamah to Zuhr, then he called the Iqamah to pray Asr, then he called the Iqamah to pray Maghrib, then he called the Iqamah to pray Isha."""
"180","""On the Day of Al-Khandaq (the battle of the Trench), Umar bin Al-Khattab came cursing the disbelievers of Quraish and said: 'O Allah's Messenger! I could not offer he Asr prayer until the sun was about to set.' The Prophet said: 'By Allah! I too have not offered the Salat.'"" So he said: ""We descended into Buthan, Allah's Messenger performed Wudu and we too performed Wudu. Allah's Messenger prayed Asr after the sun had set, then after it he prayed Maghrib."""
181,"Smurah bin Jundub narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Salatul-wusta is the Asr prayer"
182,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""I head more than one of the Companions of the Prophet; Umar bin Al-Khattab among"
"183","""I head more than one of the Companions of the Prophet; Umar bin Al-Khattab among, and he was one of the most beloved among them to me - (narrating) that Allah's Messenger prohibited the Salat after Fajr until the sun had risen, and the Salat after Asr until the sun had set."""
"184","""The Prophet only performed the two rak'ah (units of prayer) after Asr because some wealth came to him which distracted him from the two rak'ah after Zuhr, so he prayed them after Asr, then he did not repeat that."""
185,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Whoever catches a Rak'ah of Subh before the sun has risen"
"186","the Prophet said: ""Whoever catches a Rak'ah of Subh before the sun has risen, then he has caught Subh. Whoever catches a Rak'ah of Asr before the sun has set, then he has caught Asr."""
187,"Ibn Abbas narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Whoever combines two prayers without any excuse then he has committed something from the major sins"
188,"Narrated Abu Salamah, Yahya ibn Khalaf al-Basri: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Hanash, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas ṣallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said:

**""Whoever combines two prayers without having an excuse has approached one of the gates of major sins.""**

Abu 'Isa [al-Tirmidhi] commented: This Hanash is Abu 'Ali al-Rahabi, whose name is Husayn ibn Qays. He is considered weak (ḍa'īf) by the scholars of Hadith; Ahmad and others have deemed him weak.

The established practice (al-'amal) among the scholars of knowledge is that one must not combine two prayers except while traveling or at 'Arafah.

Some scholars among the Tabi'un granted a concession (rukhṣah) for combining the two prayers due to illness, and this is the position of Ahmad and Ishaq.

Other scholars stated that combining the two prayers is permissible during rain, and this is the view of al-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq.

Al-Shafi'i, however, did not permit the combining of the two prayers for the one who is ill."
189,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Sa'id ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us:** My father narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Zayd, from his father, who said:

""When we awoke in the morning, we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I informed him of the dream. He ﷺ said: **'Indeed, this is a true dream. So stand with Bilal, for his voice is more resonant and far-reaching than yours. Convey to him what was said to you, and let him call it out.'**""

He said: ""When 'Umar ibn al-Khattab heard Bilal's call to prayer, he came out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, dragging his lower garment, and saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I have seen the same as what he described.'""

He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: **'All praise is due to Allah, for that is more firmly established (athbat).'**""

He said: ""And in this chapter there is also a narration from Ibn 'Umar.""

Abu 'Isa [al-Tirmidhi] said: ""The hadith of 'Abdullah ibn Zayd is a *hasan sahih* hadith. Ibrahim ibn Sa'd also narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, more complete and longer than this one, and he mentioned in it the account of the Adhan being called in pairs (mathna mathna) and the Iqamah being called once at a time (marra marra).""

He said: ""'Abdullah ibn Zayd is Ibn 'Abd Rabbih, and it is also said Ibn 'Abd Rabb. We do not know of any narration from him from the Prophet ﷺ that is authentic except this single hadith regarding the Adhan. And 'Abdullah ibn Zayd ibn 'Asim al-Mazini has narrations from the Prophet ﷺ, and he is the paternal uncle of 'Abbad ibn Tamim."""
190,"Ibn Umar said: When the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to gather and estimate the times of the prayers, and no one would call for them. One day they spoke about this matter, and some of them said: ""Take up a bell like the bell of the Christians."" Others said: ""Take up a horn like the horn of the Jews."" Umar bin Al-Khattab said: ""Why do you not send a man to call for the prayer?"" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Bilal, get up and call for the prayer.""

Abu 'Eisa said: This Hadith is Hasan Sahih Gharib from the Hadith of Ibn Umar."
"191","Allah's Messenger sat with him and taught him the Adhan word for word. Ibrahim said, ""It is the same as our Adhan."" Bishr said: ""So I said to him, 'Repeat it to me.' So he described the Adhan with At-Tarjl'."""
192,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""Bilal was ordered to make the phrases of the Adhan even"
193,"Abdullah bin Zaid said:""Allah's Messenger would say each phrase of the call (for prayer) two times"
194,"Jabir [bin Abdullah] narrated:""Allah's Messenger said to Bilal: ""O Bilal! When you call the Adhan then do so deliberately and slowly"
"195","""Allah's Messenger said to Bilal: ""O Bilal! When you call the Adhan then do so deliberately and slowly, and when you call the Iqamah then be quick. Allow enough time between your Adhan and Iqamah for the person eating to finish what he is eating, the person drinking to finish what he is drinking, and the one who needs time to relive himself, and do not stand until you see me."""
196,"Abd ibn Humayd narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abd al-Munim, a similar narration. Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This hadith of Jabir is a hadith that we do not know except through this chain of narration, from the hadith of Abd al-Munim, and it is an unknown chain of narration (isnad majhul), and Abd al-Munim is a sheikh from Basra."
"197","""I saw Bilal calling the Adhan, and turning, and his (face) was following here and there, and his (index) fingers were in his ears, and Allah's Messenger was in a small red tent"" - ""I think,"" he (one of the narrators) said, ""it was made from a hide"" - so Bilal went out in front of him with an Anazah which he planted (in the ground) at Batha. Allah's Messenger prayed facing it, and a dog and a donkey passed in front of him; he was wearing a red Hullah, and it is as if I am now looking at the radiance of his shins."" Sufyan said: ""We think that it was a Hibrah."""
"198","""Allah's Messenger said : 'Do not say the Tathwib for any prayer except the Fajr prayer.'""

 There is something on this topic from Abu Mahdhurah.
Abu `Eisa said: We do not know of the Hadith of Bilal except as a narration of Abu Isra'il Al-Mula'i. Abu Isra'il did not hear this Hadith from Al-Hakam bin `Utaibah.

He said: He only reported it from Al-Hasan bin `Umarah, from Al-Hakam bin `Utaibah. Abu Isra'il's name is [Isma`il bin Abi Ishaq, and he is not strong according to the people of Hadith.

The people of knowledge have differed over the interpretation of At-Tathwib.

Some of them say that At-Tathwib is when one says ""As-Salatu Khairummin An-Nawm, (prayer is better than sleep)"" for the Adhan
of Fajr. This is the saying of Ibn Al-Mubarak and Ahmad.

Ishaq said something different about At-Tathwib, he said: "" is something that the people started after the Prophet; when the Mu'adh-dhin
calls the Adhan and the people are slow in coming, so between the Adhan and the Iqamah he says:
'Qad Qamatis-Salat, Hayya `Alasalat, Hayya `AlalFalah. (Prayer is ready, come to prayer, come to success.)""

 This Tathwib, which Ishaq mentioned, is the one that the people of knowledge dislike, which they innovated after the Prophet.

But Ibn Al-Mubarak and Ahmad explained that At-Tathwib is when the Mu'adh-dhin says: ""As-Salatu Khairum minan-Nawm, (prayer is better than sleep)"" for the Adhan of Fajr.
And this is the correct saying, and it is called At-Tathawwub as well, and this is the one chosen by the people of knowledge, and it is their opinion.

It has been reported from `Abdullah bin `Umar that he would say: ""As-Salatu Khairum-minan-Nawm, (prayer is better than sleep)"" for Fajr.

It has been reported from Mujahid that he said: ""I entered a Masjid with `Abdullah bin `Umar in which the Adhan was called, and we wanted to pray in it. Then the Mu'adh-dhin said the Tathwib. So `Abdullah bin `Umar left the Masjid and said: 'Let us leave the place of this innovator' And he did not pray in it.""

 `Abdullah only disliked the Tathwib that the people invented later on."
"199","""Allah's Messenger ordered me to call the Adhan for the Fajr prayer. I called the Adhan, then Bilal wanted to call the lqamah. Allah's Messenger said: 'Indeed the brother from Suda' has called the Adhan, and whoever calls the Adhan he calls the Iqamah.'"""
200,"Ibn Shihab narrated that :Abu Hurairah said: ""None should call (for the prayer except for one with Wudu)"
201,"Jabir bin Samurah narrated:The Mu'adh-dhin of Allah's Messenger would wait and he would not call the Iqamah until he saw the Allah's Messenger had come out"
"202","The Mu'adh-dhin of Allah's Messenger would wait and he would not call the Iqamah until he saw the Allah's Messenger had come out, he would call the Iqamah when he saw him."""
203,"Abu Ash-Sha'tha said:""A man exited the Masjid after the Adhan for Asr had been called. So Abu Hurairah said: 'As for this person"
204,"Malik bin Al-Huwairih said:""A cousin of mine and I arrived as guests of Allah's Messenger. He said to us: 'When you travel then call the Adhan and Iqamah and let the eldest of you lead the prayer"
"205","""A cousin of mine and I arrived as guests of Allah's Messenger. He said to us: 'When you travel then call the Adhan and Iqamah and let the eldest of you lead the prayer.'"""
206,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""The Imam is answerable and the Mu'adh-dhin is entrusted. O Allah! Guide the Imams and pardon the Mu'adh-dhins"
207,"Abu Sa'eed narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Whenyou hear the call (to prayer) then say the similar to what the Mu'adh-dhin says"
208,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him):**

He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

**""When you hear the call to prayer (Adhan), then say what the Mu'adhdhin (caller to prayer) says.""**

---

**Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said:** In this chapter (on this topic), there are narrations from Abu Rafi', Abu Hurairah, Umm Habibah, 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, 'Abdullah ibn Rabi'ah, 'A'ishah, Mu'adh ibn Anas, and Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with them all).

**Abu 'Isa said:** The Hadith of Abu Sa'id is a **Hasan Sahih** (good and authentic) Hadith.

And thus narrated Ma'mar and others from al-Zuhri, similar to the Hadith of Malik. However, 'Abdur-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated this Hadith from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurairah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). But the narration of Malik is **more authentic (asahh)**.

---

*This Hadith is recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhi and establishes the Sunnah of repeating the words of the Mu'adhdhin when one hears the Adhan.*"
"209","""Indeed, amount the last (of orders) Allah's Messenger ordered me with was to employ a Mu'adh-dhin who would not take a wage for his Adhan."""
"210","Allah's Messenger said: ""Whoever says, when he hears the Mu'adh-dhin: (Wa Ana Ashadu An La Ilaha Illallah, Wahdahu La Sharika Lahu, Wa Anna Muhammadan Abduhu Wa Rasuluhu, Radittu Billahi Rabban Wa Bil-Islam Dinan, Wa Bi Muhammadin Rasulan) 'I too testify that none has the right to be worshiped but Allah, Alone without partners, and that Muhammad is His slave and Messenger, I am pleased with Allah as my Lord, with Islam as my religion and Muhammad as a Messenger.' - Allah will pardon his sins for him."""
"211","Allah's Messenger said: ""Whoever says, when he hears the call: (Allahumma, rabba hadhihidda 'watit-tammati was Salatilqa'imah, ati Muhammadanil wasilata wal-Fadilata, wab'athhu Maqamun Mahmudan alladhi wa'adtahu) 'O Allah! Lord of this perfect call and established prayer, grant Muhammad Al-Wasilah and Al-Fadilah, and raise him to the praised station that you promised him' - then intercession on the Day of Resurrection is made lawful for him."""
212,"Narrated by Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

**""Du'a (supplication) is not rejected between the Adhan and the Iqamah.""**

Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: The hadith of Anas is a hadith that is hasan (good). It was also narrated by Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani from Burayd ibn Abi Maryam from Anas from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) with a similar narration."
"213","""On the Night of Isra, fifty prayers were made obligatory upon the Prophet. Then it was decreased until it was made five. Then it was called out: 'O Muhammad! Indeed My Word does not change; these five prayers will be recorded for you as fifty.'"""
"214","Allah's Messenger said: ""The five prayers, and Al-Jumuah (the Friday prayer) to Al-Jumuah are atonement for what is between them, as long as the major sins have not been committed."""
215,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Indeed a man's Salat in the congregation is increased by twenty five rewards over his Salat alone"
216,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Prophet said: ""I was about to order my boys to collect bundles of firewood"
"217","the Prophet said: ""I was about to order my boys to collect bundles of firewood, then order Salat to be held, then burn (the homes) of the people who did not attend the Salat."""
218,"Jabir hin Yazid hin Al-Aswad [Al-Amir] narrated that his father said:""I attended Hajj with the Prophet. I prayed the Subh (Fajr) prayer with him in Masjid AI-Khaif."" He said: ""When the Prophet finished"
"219","""I attended Hajj with the Prophet. I prayed the Subh (Fajr) prayer with him in Masjid AI-Khaif."" He said: ""When the Prophet finished, he turned (from the Qiblah) and saw two men at the back of the people who had not prayed with him. He said, 'Bring them to me.' So I brought then while they were shuddering with fear. He said: 'What prevented you from praying with us?"" They said: 'O Messenger of Allah!' We prayed at our camp.' So he said: 'Do not do that; when you pray in your camp then you come to a Masjid with a congregation, then pray with them. That will be a voluntary prayer for you.'"""
"220","""A man came when Allah's Messenger had already prayed, so he said: 'Which of you will give some reward to this person?' So a man stood to pray with him."""
"221","Allah's Messenger said: ""Whoever attends Isha (prayer) in congregation, then he has (the reward as if he had) stood half of the night. And whoever prays Isha and Fajr in congregation, then he has (the reward as if he had) spend the entire night standing (in prayer)."""
222,"Buraidah Al-Aslami narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Give glad tiding to those who walk to the Masajid in the dark; of a complete light on the Day of Resurrection"
"223","the Prophet said: ""Give glad tiding to those who walk to the Masajid in the dark; of a complete light on the Day of Resurrection."""
"224","Allah's Messenger said: ""The best rows for the men are the first of them, and the worst are the last of them. And the best rows for the women are the last of them, and the worst are the first of them."""
225,"The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""If the people knew what is in the call to prayer (Adhan) and the first row (of prayer), and found no way to attain it except by drawing lots, they would indeed draw lots for it.""

Ishaq ibn Musa al-Ansari narrated to us, saying: Ma'n narrated to us, saying: Malik narrated from Sumayy, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), a similar narration."
226,"An-Nu'man bin Bashir said:""Allah's Messenger would straighten our lines. One day he came out and saw a man whose chest was protruding from the people"
"227","""Allah's Messenger would straighten our lines. One day he came out and saw a man whose chest was protruding from the people, so he said: 'You must straighten your lines, or Allah will cause disagreement to occur among your faces."""
"228","the Prophet said: ""Let those among you with understanding and reason be close to me, then those after them, then those after them. And do not separate or dissention will occur among your hearts, and beware of the commotion of the markets."""
"229","""We prayed behind one of the Amirs, the people compelled us such that we prayed between two columns. When we had prayed, Anas bin Malik said: 'We would be prevented from this during the time of Allah's Messenger.'"""
"230","""Ziyad bin Abi Al-Ja'd took me by the hand while we were in Ar Raqqah, he made me stand before a Shaikh who was called Wabisah bin Ma'bad, from Bani Asad. Ziyad said: 'This Shaikh narrated to me that a prayed alone behind the row' - and the Shaikh was listening - 'so Allah's Messenger ordered him to repeat the Salat.'"""
231,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""I prayed with the Prophet one night. I stood at his left"
"232","""I prayed with the Prophet one night. I stood at his left, so Allah's Messenger took me by my head, from behind me, to put me on his right (side)."""
233,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""My grandmother Mutalikah invited Allah's Messenger to a meal that she prepared. He ate from it"
"234","""My grandmother Mutalikah invited Allah's Messenger to a meal that she prepared. He ate from it, then said: 'Stand so tht we may lead you in prayer.'"" He said: ""I got a Hasir of our which had become dark because of prolonged use, so I washed it with water. Allah's Messenger stood on it, and the orphan and I aligned behind him and the only lady stood behind us. He (Allah's Messenger) led us in two Rak'ah of prayer and then left."""
"235","Allah's Messenger said: ""The one who recites most of the Book of Allah is to lead the people (in prayers). If they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable in the Sunnah among them. If they are equal regarding the Sunnah, then the earliest of them to emigrate. If they are equal in their emigration then the eldest among them. And a man is not to be led in prayer in the place of his authority, and his spot of esteem in his home is not to be sat on without his permission."""
"236","the Prophet said: ""When one of you leads the people in prayer then let him be brief, for indeed here are among them the young and the old, the weak and the ill. When one of you prays alone, then let him pray as he wishes."""
237,"Abu Sa'eed narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""the key to Salat is the purification"
"238","Allah's Messenger said: ""the key to Salat is the purification, its Tahrim is the Takbir, and its Tahlilis the Taslim, and there is no Salat for one who did not recite Al-Hamd and a Surah in the obligatory (prayer) or other prayers."""
239,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""When Allah's Messenger would enter the Salat he would raise his hands while they were extended"
240,"Anas bin Malik narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Whoever performs Salat for Allah for forty days in congregation"
"241","Allah's Messenger said: ""Whoever performs Salat for Allah for forty days in congregation, catching the first Takbir, two absolutions are written for him: absolution from the Fire, and absolution from the Fire, and absolution from hypocrisy."""
"242","""When Allah;s Messenger stood for Salat during the night, he would say the Takbir (Allahu Akbar), then say: (Subhanaka Allahumma wa bihamdika wa Tabarakasmuka wa Ta'ala Jadduka wa la ilaha ghairuk.) 'Glorious You are O Allah, and with Your praise, and blesses is Your Name, and exalted is Your majesty, and none has the right to be worshipped but You' Then he would say: (A'udhu Bilahi As-Sami'il-Alimi min Ash-Shaitanir-Rajimi, min Hamzihi Wa Nafkhihi wa Nafthihi.)"" 'Allah is undoubtedly the greatest.' (Allahu Akbaru Kabira). Then he would say: 'I seek refuge in Allah the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from the cursed Shaitan, from his madness, his arrogance, and his poetry.'"
243,"Narrated by al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah and Yahya ibn Musa, who both said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Abi al-Rijal, from 'Amrah, from 'Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

""When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) began the prayer, he would say:

**'Subhanaka Allahumma wa bihamdika, wa tabaraka ismuka, wa ta'ala jadduka, wa la ilaha ghayruk.'**
(Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise is Yours. Blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no deity worthy of worship except You.)""

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: ""We do not know this hadith from 'Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) except through this chain. Harithah has been criticized regarding his memory. And Abu al-Rijal's name is Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Madani."""
"244","""While I was praying, I said: Bismillahir-Rahmanir-Rahim ""In the Name of Allah, the Merciful, the Beneficent."" My father heard me and said: ""O my son this is a newly invented matter, beware of the newly-invented.'"" He (Ibn Abdullah) said: ""I have not seen any one of the Companions of Allah's Messenger who hated a newly invented matter in Islam more than him. And he said: 'Ihave performed Salat with the Prophet, and with Abu Bakr, and Umar, and with Uthman. I did not hear any one of them saying it. So do not say it. When you are performing Salat say: Al-Hamdu lilahi Rabbil-Alamin ""All praise is due to Allah the Lord of all that exists.'"""
245,"**Narrated by Ibn ‘Abbās (raḍiya Allāhu ‘anhumā):**

""The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ‘alayhi wa sallam) used to commence his prayer with *Bismillāhir-Raḥmānir-Raḥīm* (In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful).""

**Abū ‘Īsā (al-Tirmidhī) commented:** ""This ḥadīth is not strong in its chain of narration (*isnād*). However, a number of scholars among the Companions of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ‘alayhi wa sallam) — including Abū Hurayrah, Ibn ‘Umar, Ibn ‘Abbās, and Ibn al-Zubayr — as well as those among the Successors (*tābi‘ūn*) after them, held the view of reciting the *tasmiyah* (*Bismillāhir-Raḥmānir-Raḥīm*) aloud (*jahr*). This is also the position of al-Shāfi‘ī (raḥimahullāh).""

**Note:** Ismā‘īl ibn Ḥammād is the son of Abū Sulaymān. Abū Khālid is known as Abū Khālid al-Wālibī, whose name is Hurmuz, and he is from Kūfah (Kūfan)."
246,"Ubadah bin As-Samil narrated that :the Prophet said: ""There is no Salat for the one who does not recite Fatihatil-Kitab"
247,"Wa'il bin Hujr narrated:""I heard the Prophet recite: (Not (the way) of those who earned Your anger"
248,"Bundar Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us: Yahya bin Sa'eed and Abdur-Rahman bin Mahdi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah bin Kuhail, from Hujr bin 'Anbas, from Wa'il bin Hujr, who said: ""I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) recite: '(Not of) those who earned Your anger, nor of those who went astray' and he said: 'Ameen,' and he prolonged his voice with it.""

He said: There are also narrations on this topic from 'Ali and Abu Hurairah.

Abu 'Eisa said: The Hadith of Wa'il bin Hujr is a Hasan Hadith. This is what more than one of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), the Followers, and those after them say - they hold that a man raises his voice with the Ta'min and does not conceal it. This is the view of Ash-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq.

Shu'bah narrated this Hadith from Salamah bin Kuhail, from Hujr Abu Al-'Anbas, from 'Alqamah bin Wa'il, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited: ""(Not of) those who earned Your anger, nor of those who went astray,"" and he said: ""Ameen,"" lowering his voice with it.

Abu 'Eisa said: I heard Muhammad (Al-Bukhari) say: The Hadith of Sufyan is more correct than the Hadith of Shu'bah regarding this, and Shu'bah erred in several places in this Hadith. He said ""from Hujr Abu Al-'Anbas"" when it is actually Hujr bin 'Anbas, whose surname is Abu As-Sakan. And he added in it ""from 'Alqamah bin Wa'il,"" when there is no mention of 'Alqamah in it; rather it is from Hujr bin 'Anbas, from Wa'il bin Hujr. And he said ""lowering his voice with it,"" when it is actually ""prolonging his voice with it.""

Abu 'Eisa said: I asked Abu Zur'ah about this Hadith, and he said: The Hadith of Sufyan regarding this is more correct than the Hadith of Shu'bah.

He said: Al-'Ala' bin Salih Al-Asadi narrated from Salamah bin Kuhail something similar to the narration of Sufyan."
249,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Prophet said ""When the Imam says 'Amin' then (all of you) say 'Amin.' For whoever's 'Amin' concurs with the 'Amin' of the angels"
250,"Al-Hasan narrated that :Samurah said: ""there are two pauses that I preserved from Allah's Messenger."" But Imran bin Husain rejected that and said: ""We preserved one pause."" ""So we wrote to Ubayy bin Ka'b in Al-Madinah. Ubayy wrote that Samurah was correct."" Sa'eed said: ""We said to Qatadah: 'What are those two pauses?' He said: 'When he entered into his Salat"
"251","Samurah said: ""there are two pauses that I preserved from Allah's Messenger."" But Imran bin Husain rejected that and said: ""We preserved one pause."" ""So we wrote to Ubayy bin Ka'b in Al-Madinah. Ubayy wrote that Samurah was correct."" Sa'eed said: ""We said to Qatadah: 'What are those two pauses?' He said: 'When he entered into his Salat, and when he finished his recitation.' Then he (Qatadah) said after that: 'And when he recited (Nor those who went astray.)' And he said: 'He liked to pause when he finished the recitation until he caught his breath.'"""
252,"Abdullah bin Mas'ud narrated:""Allah's Messenger would say the Takbir for every lowering and raising"
"253","""Allah's Messenger would say the Takbir for every lowering and raising, standing and sitting, and (so did) Abu Bakr and Umar."""
254,"Salim narrated from :his father (Ibn Umar) who said: ""I saw Allah's Messenger when he opened the Salat"
255,"Narrated by Qutaybah and Ibn Abi 'Umar, who both narrated from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, who said:

""I observed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) raising his hands when commencing the prayer until they were level with his shoulders, and likewise when bowing (ruku'), and when raising his head from the bowing position.""

Ibn Abi 'Umar added in his narration: ""And he (peace and blessings be upon him) did not raise his hands between the two prostrations (sajdatayn)."""
256,"Alqamah narrated that :Abdullah bin Mas'ud said: ""Shall I not demonstrate the Salat of Allah's Messenger to you?"" Then he offered Salat and he did not raise his hands except while saying the first Takbir"
"257","Abdullah bin Mas'ud said: ""Shall I not demonstrate the Salat of Allah's Messenger to you?"" Then he offered Salat and he did not raise his hands except while saying the first Takbir."""
258,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ahmad ibn Mani': Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu 'Abd ar-Rahman as-Sulami, who said:**

'Umar ibn al-Khatṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said to us: ""Indeed, the kneeling position (rukb) has been prescribed for you, so adopt the kneeling position.""

He (the narrator) said: **""In this chapter, there are narrations from Sa'd, Anas, Abu Humayd, Abu Usayd, Sahl ibn Sa'd, Muhammad ibn Maslamah, and Abu Mas'ud.""**

Abu 'Isa (at-Tirmidhi) said: **""The Hadith of 'Umar is a *hasan sahih* (good and authentic) Hadith. The practice established upon this is according to the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and the Tabi'un (Successors) and those after them. There is no disagreement among them regarding this, except for what has been narrated from Ibn Mas'ud and some of his companions that they used to place their hands together in *tadbiq* (clasping the hands upon the knees). However, *tadbiq* is considered abrogated (*mansukh*) according to the people of knowledge.""**

---

### Notes on Key Terms:

- **"
259,"Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""We used to do that, but we were forbidden from it and were commanded to place the palms upon the knees.""

Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father Sa'd, with this narration.

Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi — his name was 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn al-Mundhir.

Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi — his name was Malik ibn Rabi'ah.

Abu Hasin — his name was 'Uthman ibn 'Asim al-Asadi.

Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami — his name was 'Abd Allah ibn Habib.

Abu Ya'fur — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Ubayd ibn Nistās.

Abu Ya'fur al-'Abdi — his name was Waqid, and it is also said Waqdan, and he is the one who narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa'.

Both of them (Abu Ya'fur al-'Abdi and Abu Ya'fur 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Ubayd ibn Nistās) were from the people of Kufah."
"260","""Abu Humaid, Abu Usaid, Sahl bin Sa'd, and Muhammad bin Maslamah were once together and they were mentioning the Salat of Allah's Messenger. Abu Humaid said: 'I am the most knowledgeable among you of the Salat of Allah's Messenger: Allah's Messenger would bow and place his hands on his knees as if he was grasping them, and he would draw his forearms to hold them away from his sides.'"""
261,"Ibn Mas'ud narrated that :the Prophet said: ""When one of you bows then says while he is bowing: (Subhana Rabbiyal Azim) 'Glorious is my Lord the Magnificent' three times, then he has completed his bowing. And that is the least of it. And when he prostrates and says while prostrating: (Subhana Rabbiyal A'la) 'Glorious is my Lord the Most High' three times, then he has completed his prostrations, and that is the least of it.'"""
"262","he performed Salat with the Prophet, and that while he was bowing he would say: (Subhana Rabbiyal Azim); ""Glorious is my Lord the Magnificent"" and while prostrating: (Subhana Rabbiyal A'la) 'Glorious is my Lord the Most High.' And he would not recite an Ayah mentioning mercy, except that he would stop and ask (for mercy), and he would not recite an Ayah mentioning punishment, except that he would stop and seek refuge (with Allah from it)."
"263","from another route: ""That he performed Salat during the night with the Prophet"" and he mentioned the Hadith."
264,"Abu Mas'ud Al-Ansari [Al-Badri] narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""The Salat is not acceptable if a man is not at rest - meaning his back - while bowing and prostrating"
265,"Ali bin Abi Talib narrated:""When Allah's Messenger would raise his head from bowing he would say: (Sami Allahi liman hamidal. Rabba na lakal-hamd"
"266","""When Allah's Messenger would raise his head from bowing he would say: (Sami Allahi liman hamidal. Rabba na lakal-hamd, mil'as-samawati wa mil'al-ardi wa mil'a ma bainahuma wa mil'a ma shi'ia min shay'in ba'd) 'Allah listens to those who praise Him. O our Lord! And to You is the praise that fills the heavens and fills the earth, and fills what is between them, and fills whatever there is beyond that You will.' He said: There are narrations on this topic from Ibn Umar, Ibn Abbas, Ibn Abi Awfa, Abu Juhaifa, and Abu Sa'eed."
"267","Allah's Messenger said: ""When the Imam says:  (Sami Allahu liman hamidah) 'Allah listens to those who praise Him. Then (all of you) say: (Rabbana wa lakal-hamd) 'O our Lord! And to You is the praise for whoever's saying concurs with the saying of the angels, then his past sins will be forgiven."""
268,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Prophet said; ""Is it that one of you intends to kneel in his Salat with the kneeling of the camel?"
"269","the Prophet said; ""Is it that one of you intends to kneel in his Salat with the kneeling of the camel?"""
"270","""When the Prophet would prostrate, he placed his nose and his forehead on the ground, and he held his forearms away from his sides, and he placed his hands parallel to his shoulders."""
271,"Al-Abbas bin Abdul-Muttalib narrated that he heard :Allah's Messenger Saying: ""When the worshipper prostrates with him: His face"
272,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Prophet ordered that one prostrate on seven bones and that he not gather his hair nor his garment"
273,"Ubaidallah bin Abdullah bin Arqam Al-Khuza'I narrated from his father (Abdullah bin Arqam)"
274,"Narrated 'Abdullah bin Al-Aqram Al-Khuza'i: ""I was with my father at Al-Qa' of Namirah, and a group of riders passed by, and there was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) standing performing prayer."" He said: ""I was looking at the whiteness of his armpits when he prostrated, meaning their whiteness.""

[Abu 'Eisa said:] There are other narrations on this topic from Ibn 'Abbas, Ibn Buhainah, Jabir, Ahmar bin Jaz', Maimunah, Abu Humaid, Abu Mas'ud, Abu Usaid, Sahl bin Sa'd, Muhammad bin Maslamah, Al-Bara' bin 'Azib, 'Adi bin 'Amirah, and 'Aishah.

Abu 'Eisa said: This Ahmar bin Jaz' is a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he has only one narration.

Abu 'Eisa said: The Hadith of 'Abdullah bin Aqram is a Hasan Hadith, we do not know it except from the Hadith of Dawud bin Qais, and we do not know of 'Abdullah bin Aqram Al-Khuza'i, from the Prophet (ﷺ), other than this Hadith. This is acted upon according to most of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ).

He said: 'Abdullah bin Aqram Al-Khuza'i only has this Hadith from the Prophet (ﷺ). And 'Abdullah bin Arqam Az-Zuhri is a Companion of the Prophet (ﷺ), and he was the scribe of Abu Bakr As-Siddiq."
"275","the Prophet said: ""When one of you prostrates, then let him be balanced, and let him not lay his forearms down like the lying of the dog."""
276,"Amir bin Sa'd [bin Abi Waqqas] narrated from his father:""The Prophet ordered placing the hands (on the ground) keeping the feet erect. (by resting feet on the toes and making the tips of the toes facing the Qiblah)"
277,"(Another chain that) Amir bin Sa'd narrated:""The Prophet ordered placing the hands (on the ground)."" And he mentioned in it: ""From his father"
278,"Al-Bara bin Azib narrated:""The Salat of Allah's Messenger (was such that) when he bowed"
279,"Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Musa al-Marwazi, that Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed them, that Shu'bah informed them, from al-Hakam, from Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi Laila, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, who said:

""The prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — when he performed ruku' (bowing), and when he raised his head from ruku', and when he performed sajdah (prostration), and when he raised his head from sajdah — was approximately equal in duration.""

He (the narrator) added: ""And in this chapter (there is also a narration) from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him)."""
280,"Al-Bara said:""When we performed Salat behind Allah's Messenger"
"281","""When we performed Salat behind Allah's Messenger, he would raise his head from bowing, and no man among us would bend his back until Allah's Messenger prostrated, then we prostrated."""
282,"Tawus said:""We asked Ibn Abbas about squatting (sitting) on the heels. He said: 'It is the Sunnah.' We said: 'We think that it is difficult for a man.' He said: 'Rather"
283,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi:**

Yahya ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Tawus say:

""We said to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding *al-iq'a'* (sitting with the buttocks placed upon the heels) between the two prostrations. He said: 'It is the Sunnah.' We said: 'Indeed, we consider it to be harsh upon the man.' He said: 'Rather, it is the Sunnah of your Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).'""

Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: ""This is a *hasan sahih* hadith. Some of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) inclined toward this hadith and did not see any harm in *al-iq'a'*. This is the view of some of the people of knowledge and jurisprudence of Makkah. However, the majority of the people of knowledge dislike *al-iq'a'* between the two prostrations.""

---

**Note:** *Al-iq'a'* refers to the posture of sitting between the two prostrations (*sujood*) in prayer, wherein one sits with the buttocks resting upon the heels, with the feet placed flat on the ground. The hadith records the position of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), who affirmed this practice as being from the Sunnah of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him), while acknowledging that the majority of scholars preferred the more common posture of *al-iftirash* (sitting on the left foot with the right foot upright) between the two prostrations."
284,"Narrated by Salamah ibn Shabib, from Zayd ibn Hubab, from Kamil Abu al-'Ala', from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say between the two prostrations:

""O Allah, forgive me, have mercy upon me, mend my affairs, guide me, and provide for me."""
285,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""[Some of] the Companions of the Prophet complained [to the Prophet] about the hardship of the prostration on them"
286,"Malik bin Al-Huwairith Al-Laithi narrated that he saw :Allah's Messenger performing Salat. When he was in an odd number of his Salat"
"287","Allah's Messenger performing Salat. When he was in an odd number of his Salat, he would not get up until he had sat completely."
288,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him):**

He said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would rise during prayer upon the tops of his feet (i.e., rising on the balls of his feet).""

**Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said:** ""The Hadith of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) is acted upon according to the people of knowledge. They prefer that a man rise during prayer upon the tops of his feet.""

**Abu Isa further said:** ""Khalid ibn Ilyas is considered weak (*da'if*) by the people of Hadith. It is also said that his name is Khalid ibn Iyas.""

He added: ""Salih, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, is Salih ibn Abi Salih. Abu Salih's name is Nabhan, and he is from Madinah.""

---

## Notes on Translation

- **"
"289","""Allah's Messenger taught us, that when we sit for every two Rak'ah we should say: (At-Tahyyatulillah, was-salawatu wattayybaat. As-salamu alaika ayyuhannabiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuhu, as-salamu alaina wa ala ibadillahis-salihin. Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah wa ashadu anna Muhammadan abduhu wa Rasuluh.) 'All greetings, prayers, and pure words are Allah. Peace be upon you O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and His blessings. Peace be upon us and all of the righteous worshippers of Allah. I testify that nne has the right to be worshipped but Allah, and I testify that Muhammad is Allah's servant and Messenger.'"""
"290","""Allah's Messenger would teach us the Tashah-hud just as he would teach us the Qur'an. He would say: (At-Tahiyyatu, al mubarakatu, as-salawatu at-tayyibatulillah. Salamun alaika ayyuhan-naibiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuhu, salamun alaina wa ala ibadalillahis-salihin. Ashhadu an la ilaha illallah, wa ashhadu anna Muhammadan abduhu wa Raduluh.) 'All greetings, goodness, prayers, and pure words are for Allah. Peace be upon you O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and His blessings, Peace be upon us and all of the righteous worshippers of Allah. I testify that there is none worthy of worship except Allah, and I testify that Muhammad is Allah's worshipper and Messenger.'"""
291,"Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: ""It is from the Sunnah to recite the Tashahhud quietly.""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: ""The Ḥadīth of Ibn Masʿūd is a ḥasan gharīb ḥadīth, and the practice of the scholars is in accordance with it."""
292,"Abū Kurayb narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Idrīs narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmī narrated from his father, from Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr, who said: ""I came to Madīnah, saying to myself, 'I shall observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' When he sat for the tashahhud, he spread out his left foot and placed his left hand upon his left thigh, and he set up his right foot.""

Abū ʿĪsā said: ""This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith. The practice according to it is followed by the majority of the scholars, and it is the view of Sufyān al-Thawrī, the people of Kūfah, and Ibn al-Mubārak."""
"293","""Abu Humaid, Abu Usaid, Sahl bin Sa'd, and Muhammad bin Maslamah were once together and they were mentioning the Salat of Allah's Messenger. Abu Humaid said: ""I am the most knowledgeable among you of the Salat of Allah's Messenger: Indeed Allah's Messenger sat - meaning for Tashah-hud - he spread his left foot, and made the top of his right (foot) face the Qiblah, and he placed his right hand on his right knee, and his left hand on his left knee, and indicated with his finger - meaning the index finge.'"""
"294","""When the Prophet would sit during the Salat, he would place his right hand on his knee, and raise his finger, the one that is next to the  thumb, supplicating with it, and his left hand was spread flat on his left knee."""
"295","""The Prophet would say the Salam from his right and from his left (saying): (As-Salamu alaikum wa rahmatullah, as-Salamu alaikum wa rahmatullah) 'Peace be upon you, and Allah's mercy. Peace be upon you, and Allah's mercy.'"""
296,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah Abū Ḥafṣ al-Tinnīsī narrated to us, from Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform the *taslīm* (salutation of conclusion) in the prayer with a single *taslīm* facing his face, inclining slightly toward the right side.**

**He (Abū ʿĪsā) said:** And there are narrations on this topic from Sahl ibn Saʿd (may Allāh be pleased with him).

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** The ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) is not known to us as *marfūʿ* (elevated to the Prophet ﷺ) except through this chain of narration.

**Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl (al-Bukhārī) said:** Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad — the people of Shām (Greater Syria) narrate *munkar* (objectionable) reports from him, whereas the narrations of the people of ʿIrāq from him are more sound and more reliable.

**Muḥammad (al-Bukhārī) said:** Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: ""It is as though the Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad who came to them is not the same one from whom narrations are transmitted in ʿIrāq; it is as though he were another man whose name was confused (or altered).""

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** Some of the scholars have held this view regarding the *taslīm* in the prayer. However, the most authentic narrations from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) indicate **two *taslīms***, and upon this position stands the majority of the scholars from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), the *Tābiʿūn* (Successors), and those who came after them.

**A group among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and others held the view of a single *taslīm* in the obligatory prayer.**

**Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allāh have mercy on him) said:** ""If one wishes, he may perform a single *taslīm*, and if one wishes, he may perform two *taslīms*."""
297,"Aishah narrated:""When Allah's Messenger said the Salam he would not remain seated except long enough to say: (Allahumma antas-salam"
298,"Narrated by Ahmad ibn Mani', narrated by Mu'awiyah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) concluded the prayer with the salutation (taslim), he would not remain seated except for the duration in which he would say: 'O Allah, You are Peace, and from You comes peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of majesty and honor.'"""
299,"# Hadith Translation

Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us: Marwān ibn Mu'āwiyah al-Fazārī and Abū Mu'āwiyah narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, **through this chain of transmission**, a similar narration, and he said: **""Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour""** (*Tabārakta yā Dhā al-Jalāli wa al-Ikrām*).

He [the narrator] further stated: **In this chapter** [i.e., pertaining to this subject], there are narrations from Thawbān, Ibn ʿUmar, Ibn ʿAbbās, Abū Saʿīd, Abū Hurayrah, and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.

Abū ʿĪsā [al-Tirmidhī] said: The ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah is a **ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ** [good and authentic] ḥadīth.

Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ also narrated this ḥadīth from the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith, **in a manner similar** to the narration of ʿĀṣim.

It has also been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that **after completing the taslīm** [the salutation that concludes the prayer], he would say:

> **""Lā ilāha illallāh, waḥdahū lā sharīka lahū, lahū al-mulku wa lahū al-ḥamd, yuḥyī wa yumīt, wa huwa ʿalā kulli shayʾin qadīr. Allāhumma lā māniʿa li-mā aʿṭayta wa lā muʿṭiya li-mā manaʿta wa lā yanfādu dhā al-jaddu minka al-jaddu.""**

*""There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, alone, without partner. To Him belongs sovereignty and to Him belongs all praise. He gives life and He causes death, and He is over all things Omnipotent. O Allah, none can withhold what You have granted, and none can grant what You have withheld, and no wealth or status can benefit its owner against Your decree.""*

It has also been narrated from him ﷺ that he would say:

> **""Subḥāna Rabbika Rabb al-ʿizzati ʿammā yififūn, wa salāmun ʿalā al-mursalīn, wa al-ḥamdu lillāhi Rabb al-ʿālamīn.""**

*""Glory be to Your Lord, the Lord of Might, above what they ascribe. And peace be upon the messengers. And all praise is due to Allah, the Lord of all the worlds.""*

---

**Notes:**
- The verses cited at the conclusion correspond to **Sūrah al-Ṣāffāt (37:180–182)**.
- The supplication following the taslīm is among the well-established **adhkār** (remembrances) reported from the Prophet ﷺ after the obligatory prayers.
- Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī's grading of this ḥadīth as **ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ** reflects its high degree of authenticity within the science of ḥadīth criticism."
"300","Allah's Messenger said: ""When Allah's Messenger wanted to turn from his Salat, he would seek forgiveness from Allah three times, then say: (Allahumma Antas-Salam, wa minkas-salam, tabarakta ya dhal-jalali wal-Ikram) 'O Allah! You are the One free of defects and perfection is from You. Blessed are You, O Possesor of Majesty and Honor.'"""
301,"Rifa`ah bin Rafi` narrated:""One day Allah's Messenger was sitting in the Masjid"" Rifa'ah said: ""And we were with him. Then what appeared to be a Bedouin man entered to pray"
302,"# Hadith of Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ on the Incomplete Prayer

## Chain of Narration

ʿAlī ibn Ḥuḥr narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar informed us — from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAlī ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khallād ibn Rāfiʿ al-Zuraqī — from his grandfather — from **Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ** (may Allah be pleased with him) that:

## The Text of the Hadith

The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was one day sitting in the mosque when Rifāʿah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We were with him when a man, resembling a Bedouin, entered and prayed, performing his prayer hastily and incompletely. He then turned and gave salām to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ said:

> **""And upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed.""**

The man returned and prayed, then came back and gave salām to him. The Prophet ﷺ said:

> **""And upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed.""**

He did this two or three times; each time the man would come to the Prophet ﷺ, give salām to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ would say:

> **""And upon you — go back and pray, for you have not prayed.""**

The people were alarmed and found it distressing that one who had prayed hastily had not, in fact, prayed. Finally, the man said:

> **""Then show me and teach me, for I am but a human being who errs and sometimes gets it right.""**

The Prophet ﷺ said:

> **""Yes. When you stand for prayer, perform wuḍūʾ as Allah, the Exalted, has commanded you. Then recite the tashahhud and stand upright. If you have something of the Qurʾān, recite it; otherwise, praise Allah (ḥamd), declare His greatness (takbīr), and declare His oneness (tahlīl). Then go into rukūʿ and be calm and still in your rukūʾ. Then rise and stand upright. Then go into sujūd and be calm and still in your sujūd. Then sit and be calm and still in your sitting. Then rise. If you do all of this, your prayer is complete; and if you diminish anything from it, you have diminished from your prayer.""**

## Additional Remarks

Rifāʿah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: **""This was easier upon them than the first [ruling], for it meant that if anyone fell short in something, he would only have diminished his prayer, but his prayer would not be entirely void.""**

## Scholarly Notes

- **In the chapter** (al-bāb): there are also narrations from **Abū Hurayrah** (may Allah be pleased with him) and **ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir** (may Allah be pleased with him).

- **Abū ʿĪsā** (al-Tirmidhī, raḥimahullāh) said: **""The hadith of Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ is a ḥasan hadith.""** He further noted: ""This hadith has been narrated from Rifāʿah through more than one chain.""

---

*This hadith is recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhī and is foundational in establishing the obligation of tranquility (ṭumaʾnīnah) in the pillars of prayer as a condition for its validity.*"
303,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us:** Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd informed me, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allāh be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) entered the mosque, whereupon a man entered and performed ṣalāh. He then came and offered salām upon the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) returned his salām and said: **""Go back and perform ṣalāh, for you have not prayed.""** The man went back and performed ṣalāh in the same manner as he had prayed before. He then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and offered salām upon him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) returned his salām and said to him: **""Go back and perform ṣalāh, for you have not prayed.""** This occurred three times. The man then said: ""By the One Who has sent you with the truth, I know no better than this, so teach me."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said:

**""When you stand for ṣalāh, say the takbīr (Allāhu Akbar), then recite whatever is easy for you from the Qurʾān. Then bow (rukūʿ) until you are at rest in your bowing. Then raise your head until you stand upright. Then prostrate (sujūd) until you are at rest in your prostration. Then raise your head until you are at rest while sitting. And do this throughout all of your ṣalāh.""**

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadīth.**

He further stated: Ibn Numayr also narrated this hadīth from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, but he did not mention therein ""from his father, from Abū Hurayrah."" The narration of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar is more authentic (aṣaḥḥ). Saʿīd al-Maqburī heard directly from Abū Hurayrah and also narrated from his father from Abū Hurayrah. Abū Saʿīd al-Maqburī's name was Kaysān, and Saʿīd al-Maqburī was known by the kunya Abū Saʿd. Kaysān was a slave who was under a contract of manumisition (mukātabah) with one of them.

---

*This hadīth is famously known as the Hadīth of the Man Who Prayed Badly (Ḥadīth al-Musīʾ Ṣalātahu) and is recorded in Sunan al-Tirmidhī, as well as in the collections of al-Bukhārī and Muslim.*"
304,"Muhammad bin Bashshar and Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: Yahya bin Sa'eed Al-Qattan narrated to us, Abdul-Hamid bin Ja'far narrated to us, Muhammad bin Amr bin Ata narrated to us, from Abu Humaid As-Sa'idi who said: ""I heard him, while he was among ten of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), one of them being Abu Qatadah bin Rib'i, saying: 'I am the most knowledgeable of you regarding the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' They said: 'You were not the earliest of us in companionship with him, nor the most frequent in coming to him.' He said: 'Yes, I was.' They said: 'Then demonstrate it.' So he said: 'When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood for prayer, he stood up straight and raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders. When he wanted to bow (perform Ruku'), he raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then said: ""Allahu Akbar,"" and he bowed. Then he straightened up, not lowering his head nor raising it excessively, and placed his hands on his knees. Then he said: ""Sami' Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears whoever praises Him),"" and he raised his hands and stood straight until every bone returned to its place in a straight posture. Then he leaned down to the ground in prostration, and said: ""Allahu Akbar."" Then he kept his upper arms away from his sides, and spread out his toes. Then he folded his left leg and sat upon it, then straightened up until every bone returned to its place in a straight posture. Then he leaned down in prostration, and said: ""Allahu Akbar."" Then he folded his leg and sat, and straightened up until every bone returned to its place. Then he rose, and he did the same in the second Rak'ah. When he stood up from the two prostrations, he said ""Allahu Akbar"" and raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, just as he had done when he began the prayer. Then he continued to do likewise until, in the Rak'ah in which his prayer was to conclude, he brought his left foot forward and sat on his side in a posture of Tawarruk (sitting with the left side on the ground), then he said the Taslim (concluding salutation).'""

Abu Isa said: This Hadith is Hasan Sahih.

He said: The meaning of his statement ""and he raised his hands when he stood up from the two prostrations"" means when he stood up from the two Rak'ahs."
305,"Qutbah bin Malik narrated:""I heard Allah's Messenger reciting for Fajr: And tall date palms in the first Rak'ah"
306,"# Scholarly Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Hannād, who narrated from Wakīʿ, from Misʿar and Sufyān, from Ziyād ibn ʿAlāqah, from his uncle, Qutbah ibn Mālik, who said:**

""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite in the Fajr prayer: **'And the tall palm trees'** (Qur'an 50:10) in the first rakʿah.""

He (the narrator) said: ""And in this chapter (there are narrations) from ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, Jābir ibn Samurah, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Sāʾib, Abū Barzah, and Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with them all).""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: ""The ḥadīth of Qutbah ibn Mālik is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ ḥadīth.""

It has been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he recited Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah in the morning prayer (al-Ṣubḥ). It has also been narrated that he used to recite in the Fajr prayer from sixty verses to one hundred verses. And it has been narrated that he recited: **'When the sun is wound up'** (Sūrat al-Takwīr, 81:1). It has been narrated from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he wrote to Abū Mūsā (al-Ashʿarī) instructing him to recite the longer sūrahs of al-Mufaṣṣal in the morning prayer.

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: ""And upon this is the practice according to the people of knowledge, and this was the position of Sufyān al-Thawrī, Ibn al-Mubārak, and al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on them all).""

---

*Reference: Sunan al-Tirmidhī, Kitāb al-Ṣalāh, Bāb mā jāʾa fī al-qirāʾah fī al-Fajr.*"
307,"Umm Al-Fadl narrated:""Allah's Messenger came out to us with his head bandaged from his illness. He prayed Maghrib"
"308","""Allah's Messenger came out to us with his head bandaged from his illness. He prayed Maghrib, reciting (Surat) Al-Mursalat.""  ""He did not pray it again until he met Allah the Might and Sublime."""
309,"Al-Bara bin Azib narrated:""The Prophet would recite: By the fig and the olive for Isha"
310,"Ubadah bin As-Samit narrated:""Allah's Messenger prayed the Subh prayer"
"311","""Allah's Messenger prayed the Subh prayer, and he had difficulty with the recitation. When turned (after finishing) he said: 'I think that you are reciting behind your Imam?'"" He said: ""We said: 'Yes, Messenger of Allah, by Allah!' He said: 'Do not do that, except for Umm Al-Kitab, for there is no Salat for one who does not recite it.'"""
312,"Al-Ansari narrated to us, Ma'n narrated to us, Malik bin Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn Ukaimah Al-Laithi, from Abu Hurairah: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) finished a prayer in which he had recited aloud, and said: 'Did any of you recite along with me just now?' A man said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'I said (to myself): Why am I being contested in the Qur'an?'"" He said: ""So the people refrained from reciting along with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the prayers in which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited aloud, once they heard that from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

[Abu 'Eisa said:] There are also narrations on this topic from Ibn Mas'ud, 'Imran bin Husain, and Jabir bin 'Abdullah.

Abu 'Eisa said: This Hadith is Hasan. Ibn Ukaimah Al-Laithi's name is 'Umarah, and it is also said that it is 'Amr bin Ukaimah. Some of the companions of Az-Zuhri narrated this Hadith and mentioned this wording: Az-Zuhri said: ""So the people refrained from reciting when they heard that from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

There is nothing in this Hadith that contradicts the view of those who hold that one should recite behind the Imam, because it is Abu Hurairah himself who narrated this Hadith from the Prophet (ﷺ), and Abu Hurairah also narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: ""Whoever performs a prayer in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an (Al-Fatihah), it is deficient, it is deficient, incomplete."" So the one who conveyed this Hadith said to him: ""Sometimes I am behind the Imam."" He (Abu Hurairah) said: ""Recite it to yourself.""

Abu 'Uthman An-Nahdi narrated from Abu Hurairah, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded me to announce that there is no prayer without reciting the Opening of the Book (Al-Fatihah).""

The majority of the people of Hadith have chosen the view that a man should not recite when the Imam recites aloud, and they said: he should follow the pauses of the Imam.

The people of knowledge have differed concerning recitation behind the Imam. The majority of the people of knowledge among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), the Successors, and those after them held the view of reciting behind the Imam, and this is the view of Malik bin Anas, 'Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak, Ash-Shafi'i, Ahmad, and Ishaq. It has been narrated from 'Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak that he said: ""I recite behind the Imam, and the people recite, except for a group among the Kufans, and I hold that whoever does not recite, his prayer is still valid.""

Some of the people of knowledge were strict concerning the abandonment of the recitation of the Opening of the Book, even if one is behind the Imam, and they said: ""No prayer is valid without the recitation of the Opening of the Book, whether one is alone or behind the Imam."" They relied upon what 'Ubadah bin As-Samit narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ). 'Ubadah bin As-Samit, after the Prophet (ﷺ), used to recite behind the Imam, and he interpreted the saying of the Prophet (ﷺ): ""There is no prayer without the recitation of the Opening of the Book"" accordingly. This is the view held by Ash-Shafi'i, Ishaq, and others besides them.

As for Ahmad bin Hanbal, he said that the meaning of the saying of the Prophet (ﷺ): ""There is no prayer for the one who does not recite the Opening of the Book"" applies when one is praying alone. He argued using the Hadith of Jabir bin 'Abdullah, wherein he said: ""Whoever performs a Rak'ah in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an has not prayed, unless he is behind the Imam."" Ahmad bin Hanbal said: ""This is a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) who interpreted the saying of the Prophet (ﷺ): 'There is no prayer for the one who does not recite the Opening of the Book' to mean when one is praying alone."" Despite this, Ahmad still preferred reciting behind the Imam, and that a man should not abandon the Opening of the Book even if he is behind the Imam."
313,"Fatimah the Great narrated:""When Allah's Messenger entered the Masjid he said Salat and Salam upon Muhammad and then said: (Rabbighfirli dhunubi"
"314","""When Allah's Messenger entered the Masjid he said Salat and Salam upon Muhammad and then said: (Rabbighfirli dhunubi, waftahli abwaba rahmatik) 'O pardon my sins, and open the gates of Your mercy for me.' And when he exited he said Salat and Salam upon Muhammad, and then said: (Rabbighfirli dunubi, waftahli abwaba rakmatik) 'O Lord pardon my sins, and open the gates of Your blessings for me.'"""
"315","""I met Abdullah bin al Husain in Makkah, so I asked him about this Hadith, so he narrated it to me, he said: 'When Allah's Messenger entered, he said (Rabbi aftahli abwaba rahmatik) 'O Lord, open the gates of Your mercy for me.' And when he exited he said: (Rabbi iftahli abwaba fadlik) 'O Lord, open the gates of Your blessings for me.'"""
316,"Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""All of the earth is a Masjid except for the graveyard and the washroom"
317,"Uthman bin Affan narrated that :he heard Allah's Messenger say: ""Whoever builds a Masjid for (the sake of) Allah"
"318","he heard Allah's Messenger say: ""Whoever builds a Masjid for (the sake of) Allah, then Allah will build a similar house for him in Paradise."""
"319","the Prophet said: ""Whoever builds a Masjid for (the sake of) Allah, be it small or large, then Allah will build a house for him in Paradise."""
320,"Ibn Umar narrated:""We would sleep in the Masjid during the time of Allah's Messenger and we were young men"
321,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with both of them):**

He said: ""We used to sleep in the mosque during the time of the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) while we were young men.""

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** The Hadith of Ibn ʿUmar is a *ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ* Hadith.

A group of the people of knowledge granted permission for sleeping in the mosque. Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with both of them) said: ""One should not take it as a place of overnight lodging (*mabīt*) nor as a place of midday rest (*maqīl*)."" And a group of the people of knowledge inclined toward the statement of Ibn ʿAbbās.

---

**Source:** *Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī*"
322,"Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri narrated:""A man from Banu Khudrah and a man from Banu Amr bin Awf were disputing about the Masjid that was founded upon Taqwa. The man from Banu Khudrah said: 'It is the Masjid of Allah's Messenger.' The other one said that it was Masjid Qub. So they went to ask Allah's Messenger about that. He said: 'It is this - meaning his Masjid - 'and in that one (Masjid Quba) there is much good"
"323","""A man from Banu Khudrah and a man from Banu Amr bin Awf were disputing about the Masjid that was founded upon Taqwa. The man from Banu Khudrah said: 'It is the Masjid of Allah's Messenger.' The other one said that it was Masjid Qub. So they went to ask Allah's Messenger about that. He said: 'It is this - meaning his Masjid - 'and in that one (Masjid Quba) there is much good.'"""
324,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Salat is this Masjid of mine is better than a thousand Salat in another"
325,"Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""A mount is not saddled (for a journey) except to three Masajid: Al-Masjid Al-Haram"
"326","Allah's Messenger said: ""A mount is not saddled (for a journey) except to three Masajid: Al-Masjid Al-Haram, this Masjid of mine, and Masjid Al-Aqsa.""

 This Hadith is Hasan Sahih."
"327","Allah's Messenger said: ""When the Iqamah is called for Salat do not come to it rushing, rather come to it walking, and while you have tranquility. What you catch of it then pray it, and what you missed of it, then complete it."""
328,"Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Khallāl narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Maʿmar informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, a narration similar in meaning to the ḥadīth of Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah. Thus did ʿAbd al-Razzāq state it, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. And this is more authentic than the ḥadīth of Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ."
329,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""One of you does not cease to be in Salat as long as he is waiting for it. And the angels do not cease praying for one of you as long as he remains in the Masjid (saying): 'Allah! Forgive him. O Allah! Have mercy upon him' - as long as he does not commit Hadath."" A man from Hadramawt said: ""And just what is Hadath Abu Hurairah?"" He said: ""Breaking wind"
"330","Allah's Messenger said: ""One of you does not cease to be in Salat as long as he is waiting for it. And the angels do not cease praying for one of you as long as he remains in the Masjid (saying): 'Allah! Forgive him. O Allah! Have mercy upon him' - as long as he does not commit Hadath."" A man from Hadramawt said: ""And just what is Hadath Abu Hurairah?"" He said: ""Breaking wind, or passing gas."""
331,"Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu al-Ahwāṣ narrated to us — from Simāk ibn Harb — from ʿIkrimah — from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: **""The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, would pray upon the *khumrah* (prayer mat).""**

He further stated: ""In this chapter, there are also narrations from Umm Ḥabībah, Ibn ʿUmar, Umm Sulaym, ʿĀʾishah, Maymūnah, and Umm Kulthūm bint Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad — though she did not hear it directly from the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam — as well as from Umm Salamah.""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: ""The ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās is a *ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ* (sound and authentic) ḥadīth."" He added: ""This is the position held by some of the scholars."" Aḥmad and Isḥāq stated: ""It has been authentically established from the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, that he prayed upon the *khumrah*.""

Abū ʿĪsā further clarified: ""The *khumrah* is a small mat (woven from palm leaves)."""
332,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""Allah's Messenger used to mingle with us such that he said to my younger brother: 'O Abu Umair! What did the Nughair do?'"" He (Anas) said: ""A Bisat of ours would be sprinkled (with water) to perform Salat on"
"333","""Allah's Messenger used to mingle with us such that he said to my younger brother: 'O Abu Umair! What did the Nughair do?'"" He (Anas) said: ""A Bisat of ours would be sprinkled (with water) to perform Salat on."""
334,"Muba bin Talhah narrated from his father (Talhah) that :Allah's Messenger said: ""When one of you placed something like the post (handle) of the camel saddle in front of him"
335,"Zaid bin Khalid Al-Juhni sent a message to Abu Juhaim asking him what he had heard from Allah's Messenger about passing in front of a person who was performing Salat. Abu Juhaim said :that Allah's Messenger said: ""If the one who passed in front of the person performing Salat knew what he was doing"
336,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""I was a companion rider on a female donkey with Al-Fadl. We came while the Prophet and his Companions were performing Salat at Mina."" He said: ""We dismounted from it and joined the row. The donkey then passed in front of them"
"337","""I was a companion rider on a female donkey with Al-Fadl. We came while the Prophet and his Companions were performing Salat at Mina."" He said: ""We dismounted from it and joined the row. The donkey then passed in front of them, and this did not invalidate their Salat."""
"338","Allah's Messenger said: ""When a man performs Salat, and there is nothing in front of him like the post of a saddle, or a camel saddle, then his Salat is severed by (passing of) a black dog, a woman, and a donkey."" It was said to Abu Dharr: ""What is the problem with the black dog rather than the red or white one?"" He said: ""O my nephew! I asked Allah's Messenger just as you have asked me. He said: 'The black dog is a Shaitan (devil).'"""
339,"Al-Bara bin Azib narrated:""When Allah's Messener arrived in Al-Madinah"
"340","""When Allah's Messener arrived in Al-Madinah, he faced Bait Al-Maqdis in Salat for sixteen or seventeen months. Allah's Messenger longed to face the direction of the Ka'bah, so Allah Most High revealed: Verily! We have seen the turning of your face towards the heaven. Surely We shall turn you o a Qiblah that shall please you. So turn  your face in the direction of Al-Masjid Al-Haram.So he faced the Ka'bah, and he liked that. A man performed the Asr prayer with him, then passed by some of the Ansar who were bowing in Salat for Asr while facing Bait Al-Maqdis."" He told them that he had faced the direction of the Ka'bah, so they changed (their direction) while they were bowing."" 
 There are narrations on this topic from Ibn Umar, Ibn Abbas, Umarah bin Aws, Amr bin Awf Al-Muzani and Anas."
341,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""What is between the east and the west is Qiblah"
342,"Abu Hurairah narrated:(Another chain with a similar narration)"
343,"(Another chain narrating that) Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""What is between the east and the west is Qiblah"
344,"Abdullah bin Amir bin Rabi'ah narrated from his father who said:""We were with the Prophet on a journey on a very dark night and we did not know the direction of the Qiblah. So each man among us prayed in his own direction. In the morning when we mentioned that to the Prophet"
"345","""We were with the Prophet on a journey on a very dark night and we did not know the direction of the Qiblah. So each man among us prayed in his own direction. In the morning when we mentioned that to the Prophet, then the following was revealed: So where ever you turn, there is the Face of Allah."""
346,"# Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Ibn ʿUmar** (may Allāh be pleased with both of them) that the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) **forbade** (praying) in **seven places**: the **dunghill** (al-Mazbalah), the **slaughterhouse** (al-Majzarah), the **graveyard** (al-Maqbarah), the **middle of the road** (Qāriʿat al-Ṭarīq), the **bathhouse** (al-Ḥammām), the **camel pens** (resting places of camels) (Maʿāṭin al-Ibil), and **upon the roof of the House of Allāh** (the Sacred Mosque).

---

## Chain of Narration (Isnād)

- **Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān** → **al-Muqriʾ** → **Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb** → **Zayd ibn Jabīrah** → **Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn** → **Nāfiʿ** → **Ibn ʿUmar** (may Allāh be pleased with both of them).

---

## Notes

This Hadith establishes the prohibition of performing ṣalāh (prayer) in locations deemed impure, disrespectful, or inappropriate for worship. The seven locations cited include places of filth, slaughter, burial, public thoroughfares, bathhouses, areas where camels gather, and the roof of the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Ḥarām). The ruling reflects the principle of maintaining the sanctity and purity required for prayer, as well as avoiding places associated with impurity (najāsah) or disrespect to sacred spaces."
347,"Abu Hurairah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""Perform Salat in sheep pens but do not perform Salat in the camels' resting area"
348,"Abu Kurayb narrated to us: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Pray in the resting places of sheep, but do not pray in the watering places of camels."""
349,"Anas bin Malik narrated:""The Prophet would perform Salat in sheep pens"
350,"Jabir narrated:""The Prophet dispatched me for something he needed. I came to him while he was performing Salat on his mount"
"351","""The Prophet dispatched me for something he needed. I came to him while he was performing Salat on his mount, heading east, he made the prostrations lower than the bowing."""
"352","""The Prophet performed Salat towards his she-camel, or his mount, and he would perform Salat while on his mount, whichever direction it was facing."""
353,"Ibn Umar narrated that:the Prophet said: ""When the supper is presented and the Iqamah is called for Salat"
354,"Aishah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""When one of you is sleepy and he is performing Salat"
"355","Allah's Messenger said: ""When one of you is sleepy and he is performing Salat, then let him lie down until the sleep is gone from him. For when one of you performs Salat while he is sleepy, perhaps he wants to seek forgiveness but he curses himself."""
"356","a man among them said: ""Malik bin Al-Huwairith came to us in our Musalla to narrate. One day when it was time for Salat, we told him to go ahead (to lead the prayer). He said: 'Let one of you go forward, until I narrate to you why I would not go forward (to lead prayer): I heard Allah's Messenger saying: ""Whoever visits a people, then he does not lead them, but a man among them leads them."""
"357","the Prophet said: ""It is not allowed for a man to look into the interior of a man's house until he has been given permission, for if he looks, then he has entered. And one who leads people (in Salat) should not supplicate for himself alone with the exclusion of his congregation. If he does, then he has betrayed them. And one is not to stand for Salat while he has to urinate."""
"358","""Allah's Messenger cursed three people: A man who leads people (in Salat) while they dislike him, a woman who spends a night while her husband is angry with her, and a man who hears: 'Hayya Alal Falah (come to success)' then does not respond."""
359,"Abu Umamah narrated that :Allah's Messenger said: ""There are three whose Salat would not rise up beyond their ears: The runaway slave until he returns"
"360","Allah's Messenger said: ""There are three whose Salat would not rise up beyond their ears: The runaway slave until he returns, a woman who spends a night while her husband is angry with her, and a people's Imam whom they dislike."""
"361","""Allah's Messenger fell from a horse and got injured, so he led Salat sitting and we also offered Salat sitting. When he completed the Salat he said: ""The Imam is appointed to be followed; when he says the Takbir then say the Takbir, when he bows, then bow, and when he raises his head, then raise your heads. When he says: ""Sami' Allahu liman hamidah (Allah listens to those who praise him)"" then say: ""Rabbana wa lakal-hamd. (O our Lord! And all praise is Yours.)"" and when he prostrates, then prostrate, and when he performs Salat sitting, then pray sitting altogether.'"""
362,"Anas narrated:""Allah's Messenger performed Salat during his illness behind Abu Bakr while seated"
363,"Anas narrated:""Allah's Messenger performed Salat during his illness behind Abu Bakr while seated, wrapped in a garment."""
364,"Ziyad bin Haqah narrated:""Al-Mughirah bin Shu'bah les us in Salat. When he prayed two Rak'ah he stood without sitting. Those who were behind him said 'Subhan Allah' and he indicated to them that they should stand. He said the Taslim when he had finished his Salat and he performed two prostrations of As-Sahw"
365,"Ubaidullah bin Abdullah bin Mas'ud narrated from his father (Abdullah bin Mas'ud) that he said:""When Allah's Messenger sat for the first two Rak'ah it was as if he was on hot stones."" Shu'bah (one of the narrators) said: ""Then Sa'd's lips moved (saying) something. So I said: 'Until he stood?' He said: 'Until he stood"
"366","""When Allah's Messenger sat for the first two Rak'ah it was as if he was on hot stones."" Shu'bah (one of the narrators) said: ""Then Sa'd's lips moved (saying) something. So I said: 'Until he stood?' He said: 'Until he stood.'"""
367,"Ibn Umar narrated:""I said to Bilal: 'How did the Prophet (S) reply to them when they gave him Salams and he was performing Salat?' He said: 'He would motion with his hand"
"368","""I said to Bilal: 'How did the Prophet (S) reply to them when they gave him Salams and he was performing Salat?' He said: 'He would motion with his hand.'"""
369,"Abu Hurairah narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:""Yawning in prayer (salah) is from the Shaitan"
370,"Imran bin Husain narrated:""I asked Allah's Messenger (S) about the Salat for a man who is sitting. He said: 'Whoever performs Salat while standing then that is more virtuous. And whoever performs Salat while sitting"
"371","""I asked Allah's Messenger (S) about the Salat for a man who is sitting. He said: 'Whoever performs Salat while standing then that is more virtuous. And whoever performs Salat while sitting, then he gets half the rewards of the one standing, and whoever performs Salat while lying down, then he gets half the rewards of the one sitting.'"""
372,"This Hadith has been narrated through this chain of narration from Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān, except that he narrates it from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, about the prayer of a sick person. He said: 'Pray standing; if you are unable, then sitting; and if you are unable, then on your side.'""

Hannād narrated to us, saying: Wakīʿ narrated to us from Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim, with this Hadith.

Abū ʿĪsā said: ""We do not know of anyone who narrated from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim in a manner similar to the narration of Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān. However, Abū Usāmah and others narrated from Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim in a manner similar to the narration of ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus.""

The meaning of this Hadith, according to some scholars, pertains to the voluntary (nafl) prayer.

Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated to us from Ashʿath ibn ʿAbd al-Malik from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""A man may pray the voluntary prayer standing, sitting, or lying down, if he so wishes.""

The scholars have differed regarding the prayer of a sick person who is unable to pray while sitting. Some scholars said: ""He should pray on his right side."" Others said: ""He should pray lying on his back with his feet directed toward the Qiblah.""

Sufyān al-Thawrī said regarding this Hadith: ""Whoever prays sitting receives half the reward of one who prays standing."" He clarified: ""This ruling applies to a healthy person who has no excuse, specifically in the case of voluntary prayers. However, whoever has an excuse, such as illness or otherwise, and prays sitting, he receives the same reward as one who prays standing."" This opinion of Sufyān al-Thawrī has been supported by narrations found in some versions of this Hadith."
373,"Aishah narrated:""The Prophet (S) would perform Salat while sitting. And he would recite while sitting. When about thirty or forty Ayah of his recitation remained he would standup and recite while he was standing"
374,"Narrated by al-Ansari, narrated by Ma'n, narrated by Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah, from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray while sitting, reciting while seated. When approximately thirty or forty verses of his recitation remained, he would stand and recite while standing, then bow and prostrate. He would do the same in the second rak'ah.

Abu Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: This is a hasan sahih hadith."
"375","""I asked her about the voluntary Salat of Allah's Messenger (S), she said: 'He would pray long into the night standing, and long into the night sitting. So when he recited and he was standing, he would bow and prostrate whole he was standing, and when he recited while he was sitting, he bowed and prostrated while he was sitting.'"""
376,"Aishah narrated that:Allah's Messenger said: ""The Salat of a women who has reached the age of menstruation is not accepted without a Khimar"
"377","Allah's Messenger said: ""The Salat of a women who has reached the age of menstruation is not accepted without a Khimar"""
378,"Abu Dharr narrated that :the Prophet (S) said: ""When one of you stands for Salat then he should not smoothen the pebbles"
379,"Mu'aiqib narrated:""I asked Allah's Messenger (S) about smoothening the pebbles during Salat. He said: 'If you must do so"
380,"Umm Salamah narrated:""The Prophet (S) saw a boy of ours - called Aflah - blowing when he prostrated. So he said: 'O Aflah! Put your face in the dirt"
"381","""The Prophet (S) saw a boy of ours - called Aflah - blowing when he prostrated. So he said: 'O Aflah! Put your face in the dirt.'"""
382,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""The Prophet (S) prohibited than a man to pray with his hands on his hip"
383,"Abu Rafi narrated that :He passed by Al-Hasan bin Ali while he was performing Salat and he had gathered his locks at the back of his head"
"384","He passed by Al-Hasan bin Ali while he was performing Salat and he had gathered his locks at the back of his head, so he (Abu Rafi) undid them, and Al-Hasan turned to him angrily. He said: ""Resume your Salat and do not be angry, for indeed I heard Allah's Messenger (S) saying: 'That is the seat of Ash-Shaitan.'"""
"385","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""As-Salat is two and two, with a Tashahhud for every two Rak`ah, with humility, imploring, having a sense of tranquility, pleading and showing helplessness and stretching out your hand"" - he said: raising them - ""to your Lord, with the insides of them facing your face, saying: 'O Lord! O Lord! And whoever does not do this, then it is like this or that."""
"386","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When one of you performs Wudu and does so well, then he leaves intending to go to the Masjid, then let him not intertwine his fingers, for indeed he is in Salat."""
387,"Ma'dan bin Talhah Al Yamari said:""I met Thawban the freed slave of Allah's Messenger (S) and said to him"
388,"Narrated to us was Abu 'Ammār, to whom narrated al-Walīd, who said — and narrated to us Abū Muḥammad Rajā', who said — narrated to us al-Walīd ibn Muslim, from al-Awzā'ī, who said — narrated to me al-Walīd ibn Hishām al-Mu'ayṭī, who said — narrated to me Ma'dān ibn Ṭalḥah al-Ya'marī, who said:

I met Thawbān, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I said to him: ""Direct me to a deed by which Allah will benefit me and through which He will admit me to Paradise."" He remained silent for a long while, then turned toward me and said: ""Upon you is prostration (sujūd), for indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:

*'There is no servant who prostrates to Allah, a single prostration, except that Allah raises him thereby a degree and effaces thereby a sin from him.'*""

**Note:** The chain of narration (isnād) contains a dual transmission path, both converging through al-Walīd ibn Muslim from al-Awzā'ī, reflecting the rigorous methodology of the Muhaddithīn in preserving the Prophetic traditions."
"389","""I met Abu Ad-Darda, and I asked him what I had asked Thawban, so he said: 'Perform prostrations, for I heard Allah's Messenger saying: 'No worshipper performs a prostration to Allah except that by it Allah will raise him a level, and erase a sin from him for it.'"""
"390","""Allah's Messenger (S) ordered killing the two black things in Salat; the snake and the scorpion."""
391,"Ibn Abbas narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Jibril (peace be upon him) led me (in Salat) twice at the House. So he prayed Zuhr the first time when the shadow was similar to (the length of) the strap a sandal. Then he prayed Asr when everything was similar (to the length of) its shadow. Then he prayed Maghrib when the sun had set and the fasting person breaks fast. Then he prayed Isha when the twilight had vanished. Then he prayed Fajr when Fajr (dawn) began"
392,"Muhammad bin Bashshar narrated to us, Abdul A'la and Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya bin Abi Kathir, from Muhammad bin Ibrahim, that Abu Hurairah and As-Sa'ib Al-Qari used to perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness before the Taslim.

Abu Isa said: The Hadith of Ibn Buhainah is a Hasan Sahih Hadith. This is acted upon according to some of the people of knowledge, and it is the view of Ash-Shafi'i, who holds that all prostrations of forgetfulness are before the Salam. He says that this abrogates the other Hadiths, and he mentions that the last action of the Prophet (ﷺ) was according to this.

Ahmad and Ishaq said: When a man stands up after two Rak'ah (mistakenly, before completing the prayer), he should perform the two prostrations of forgetfulness before the Salam, based on the Hadith of Ibn Buhainah.

Abdullah Ibn Buhainah is Abdullah bin Malik; he is the son of Buhainah - Malik is his father and Buhainah is his mother. This is how Ishaq bin Mansur informed me, from Ali bin Abdullah Al-Madini.

Abu Isa said: The people of knowledge differed regarding the two prostrations of forgetfulness, as to when a man should perform them - before the Salam or after it. Some of them held the view that they should be performed after the Salam, and this is the view of Sufyan Ath-Thawri and the people of Kufah. Others held that they should be performed before the Salam, and this is the view of most of the jurists among the people of Madinah, such as Yahya bin Sa'eed, Rabi'ah, and others, and it is what Ash-Shafi'i says.

Others said: If it is due to an addition in the prayer, then it is after the Salam, and if it is due to a deficiency, then it is before the Salam. This is the view of Malik bin Anas.

Ahmad said: What has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the two prostrations of forgetfulness should each be applied according to its context. He holds that if one stands up after two Rak'ah, then according to the Hadith of Ibn Buhainah, he should perform them before the Salam. And if he prays Zuhr with five Rak'ah, then he should perform them after the Salam. And if he says the Salam after two Rak'ah of Zuhr or Asr, then he should perform them after the Salam. Each is to be applied according to its context. And every case of forgetfulness for which there is no report from the Prophet (ﷺ) - then the two prostrations of forgetfulness are to be performed before the Salam.

Ishaq said something similar to the view of Ahmad in all of this, except that he said: every case of forgetfulness for which there is no report from the Prophet (ﷺ) - if it is due to an addition in the prayer, he performs them after the Salam, and if it is due to a deficiency, he performs them before the Salam."
"393","""The Prophet (S) prayed five (Rakah) for Zuhr, so it was said to him: 'Has the prayer been added to, or have you merely forgotten?' So he (S) performed two prostrations after he had said the Salam."""
394,"Abu Hurairah narrated:""The Prophet (S) performed two prostrations after the Salam"
395,"Abu Al-Muhallab narrated from Imran bin Husain that :the Prophet (S) led them in Salat he forgot (something) so he performed two prostrations"
396,"Iyad bin Hilal said:""I said to Abu Sa'eed: 'One of us performs Salat and we do not know how (much) we prayed.' So he said: 'Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When one of you performs Salat and he does now know how (much) he prayed then let him perform two prostrations while he is sitting"
397,"Abu Hurairah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Indeed the Shaitan comes to one of you in his Salat confusing him until he does now know how much he has prayed. When one of you experiences that then let him perform two prostrations while sitting"
"398","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Indeed the Shaitan comes to one of you in his Salat confusing him until he does now know how much he has prayed. When one of you experiences that then let him perform two prostrations while sitting."""
"399","""When one of you becomes forgetful in his Salat so that he does not know if he prayed one or two, then let him consider it one. When he does not know if he prayed three or four, then let him consider it three, and let him perform two prostrations before he says the Taslim."""
"400","""The Prophet (S) turned (finished the prayer) after two (Rak'ah), so Dhul-Yadain said: 'Has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten O Messenger of Allah?"" The Prophet (S) said: 'Is what Dhul-Yadain said the truth?' The people said yes,  so Allah's Messenger (S) stood to perform the last two (Rakah) of Salat, then he said the Taslim. Then he said the Takbir and prostrated in a manner the same or longer than his (normal) prostrations."""
401,"Al-Bara bin Azib narrated:""The Prophet (S) would perform the Qunut in the Subh and Maghrib prayers"
402,"Abu Malik Al-Ashjai narrated:""I said to my father: 'O my father! You offered Salat behind Allah's Messenger (S)"
"403","""I said to my father: 'O my father! You offered Salat behind Allah's Messenger (S), Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali bin Abi Talib here in Al-Kufah for about five years. Did they say the Qunut?' He said: 'It is a newly invented matter my son.'"""
404,"Muadh bin Rifa'ah narrated that his father said:""I prayed behind Allah's Messenger (S). I sneezed and said: Al-Hamdulillah"
"405","""I prayed behind Allah's Messenger (S). I sneezed and said: Al-Hamdulillah, hamdan kathiran tayyiban mubarakan fih, mubarakan alaihi kama yuhibbu Rabbana Wa Yarda (All praise is due to Alah, many good blessed praises, blessings for Him as our Lord loves and is pleased with.) When Allah's Messenger (S) prayed and turned (after finishing) he said: 'Who was the speaker during the Salat?' No one spoke. Then he said it a second time: 'Who was the speaker during the Salat?' But no one spoke. Then he said it a third time: 'Who was the speaker during the Salat?'"" So Rifa'ah bin Rafi bin Afra said: ""It was I, O Messenger of Allah (S)."" He said: ""What did you say?"" He said: ""I said: 'Al-Hamdulillah, hamdan kathiran tayyiban mubarakan fih, mubarakan alaihi kama yuhibbu Rabbana Wa Yarda. The Prophet (S) said: ""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul! I saw thirty-some angels competing over which of then would ascend with it."""
"406","""We used to talk behind Allah's Messenger (S) during the Salat, a man among us would talk to his companions next to him until (the following) was revealed: And stand before Allah with obedience. (2:238) So we were ordered to be silent and prohibited from talking."""
"407","""I heard Ali saying: 'Indeed I am a man who, when I heard a Hadith from Allah's Messenger (S) then Allah causes me to benefit from it as much as He wills for me to benefit from it. When a man among his Companions narrated to me I ask him to swear an oath to me about it, and when he swears an oath to me I trust him. And Abu Bakr narrated to me - and Abu Bakr told the truth - he said: ""I heard Allah's Messenger (S) saying: 'There is no man who commits a sin, then makes Wudu, then performs Salat, then seeks forgiveness from Allah, except that Allah forgives him.' Then he recited this Ayah: Those who when they have committed Fahishah or wronged themselves with eveil, remember Allah. (3:135) until the end of the Ayah."""
408,"Abdullah bin Amr narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When he commits Hadath - meaning a man - and he is sitting in the end of his Salat before saying the Taslim"
"409","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When he commits Hadath - meaning a man - and he is sitting in the end of his Salat before saying the Taslim, then his Salat is acceptable."""
410,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""Some of the poor people came to Allah's Messenger (S) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah (S)! The rich pray as we pray"
411,"Narrated Ibn 'Abbas: ""The poor came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Indeed the wealthy pray as we pray, and they fast as we fast, and they have wealth with which they free slaves and give charity.' He said: 'So when you pray, say: Allahu Akbar thirty-four times, and Alhamdulillah thirty-three times, and Subhan Allah thirty-three times, and La ilaha illallah ten times. For indeed by it you will catch up to those who preceded you, and none who come after you will catch up to you.'""

[Abu 'Eisa said:] There are other narrations on this topic from Ka'b bin 'Ujrah, Anas, 'Abdullah bin 'Amr, Zaid bin Thabit, Abu Ad-Darda, Ibn 'Umar, and Abu Dharr.

[Abu 'Eisa said:] The Hadith of Ibn 'Abbas is a Hasan Gharib Hadith.

There is also something on this topic from Abu Hurairah and Al-Mughirah. And it has been related from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: ""There are two habits which no Muslim man maintains but that he enters Paradise: He glorifies Allah after every prayer ten times, and praises Him ten times, and magnifies Him ten times, and he glorifies Allah when he goes to sleep thirty-three times, and praises Him thirty-three times, and magnifies Him thirty-four times."""
"412","They were with the Prophet (S) on a journey. They wound up in a narrow area when Salat became due. Then it began raining from the sky above them, and it was wet beneath them. So Allah's Messenger (S) called the Adhan while he was on his mount, and then the Iqamah, going forward on his mount. He let them in Salat by making gestures, making his prostrations lower than his bowing."
413,"Huraith bin Qabisah narrated:""I arrived in Al-Madinah and said: 'O Allah! Facilitate me to be in a righteous gathering.'"" He said: ""I sat with Abu Hurairah and said: 'Indeed I asked Allah to provide me with a righteous gathering. So narrate a hadith to me which you heard from Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) so that perhaps Allah would cause me to benefit from it.' He said: 'I heard Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) say: ""Indeed the first deed by which a servant will be called to account on the Day of Resurrection is his Salat. If it is complete"
"414","""I arrived in Al-Madinah and said: 'O Allah! Facilitate me to be in a righteous gathering.'"" He said: ""I sat with Abu Hurairah and said: 'Indeed I asked Allah to provide me with a righteous gathering. So narrate a hadith to me which you heard from Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) so that perhaps Allah would cause me to benefit from it.' He said: 'I heard Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) say: ""Indeed the first deed by which a servant will be called to account on the Day of Resurrection is his Salat. If it is complete, he is successful and saved, but if it is defective, he has failed and lost. So if something is deficient in his obligatory (prayers) then the Lord, Mighty and Sublime says: 'Look! Are there any voluntary (prayers) for my worshipper?' So with them, what was deficient in his obligatory (prayers) will be completed. Then the rest of his deeds will be treated like that."""
"415","""Whoever is regular with twelve Rak'ah of Sunnah (prayer), Allah will build a house for him in Paradise: Four Rak'ah before Zuhr, two Rak'ah after it, two Rak'ah after Maghrib, two Rak'ah after Isha, and two Rak'ah before Fajr."""
"416","""Whoever prays twelve Rak'ah in a day and night, a house will be built from him in Paradise: Four Rak'ah before Zuhr, two Rak'ah after it, two Rak'ah after Maghrib, two Rak'ah after Isha, and two Rak'ah before Fajr in the morning Salat."""
417,"Ibn Umar narrated:""I watched the Prophet (S) for a month. In the two Rak'ah before Fajr he would recite: Say: ""O you disbelievers!"" and Say: ""Allah is One"
418,"Aishah narrated:""When Allah's Messenger (S) prayed the two Rak'ah (before) Fajr if he needed something from me he would talk to me"
419,"Ibn Umar narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""There is no Salat after Al-Fajr (begins) except two prostrations"
420,"Abu Hurairah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When one of you prays the two Rak'ah of Fajr then let him lay down on his right (side)"
421,"Abu Hurairah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""When the Iqamah has been called for the Salat then there is no Salat except the obligatory"
422,"Qais narrated:""Allah's Messenger (S) went out and the Iqamah was called for the Salat so I prayed As-Subh with him. Then the Prophet (S) turned and found me performing Salat so he said: 'Easy O Qais! Are there two prayers together?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! I did not perform the two Rak'ah (before) Fajr.' He said: 'Then there is no harm"
"423","""Allah's Messenger (S) went out and the Iqamah was called for the Salat so I prayed As-Subh with him. Then the Prophet (S) turned and found me performing Salat so he said: 'Easy O Qais! Are there two prayers together?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah! I did not perform the two Rak'ah (before) Fajr.' He said: 'Then there is no harm.'"""
"424","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Whoever did not pray the two Rak'ah (before) Fajr then let him pray them after the sun has risen."""
425,"Ibn Umar narrated:""I prayed two Rak'ah with the Prophet (S) before Az-Zuhr and two Rak'ah after it"
426,"Aishah narrated:""When the Prophet (S) would not perform the four Rak'ah before Az-Zuhr he would pray them after it"
427,"Umm Habibah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Whoever prays four before Az-Zuhr and four after"
428,"Umm Habibah the wife of the Prophet (S) narrated that:She heard Allah's Messenger (S) saying: ""Whoever maintains four Rak'ah before Az-Zuhr and four after it"
429,"Ali narrated:""Allah's Messenger (S) would pray four Rak'ah before Al-Asr separating between them with At-Taslim upon the angels that are close (to Allah) and those who follow them among the Muslims and the believers"
"430","""Allah's Messenger (S) would pray four Rak'ah before Al-Asr separating between them with At-Taslim upon the angels that are close (to Allah) and those who follow them among the Muslims and the believers."""
431,"Abdullah bin Mas'ud narrated:""I can not enumerate (how many times) I heard Allah's Messenger (S) reciting - in the two Rak'ah after Al-Maghrib and the two Rak'ah before Salatul-Fajr with: Say: ""O you disbelievers!"" and Say: ""He is Allah the One"
"432","""I can not enumerate (how many times) I heard Allah's Messenger (S) reciting - in the two Rak'ah after Al-Maghrib and the two Rak'ah before Salatul-Fajr with: Say: ""O you disbelievers!"" and Say: ""He is Allah the One."""
433,"Ibn Umar narrated:""I memorized ten Rak'ah from Allah's Messenger (S) which he would pray in a night and a day: Two Rak'ah before Az-Zuhr"
434,"Narrated by al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī al-Khallāl, who narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, who reported from Maʿmar, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said:

""I preserved from the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) ten rakʿahs that he used to perform during the night and the day: two rakʿahs before the Ẓuhr prayer, two after it, two after the Maghrib prayer, and two after the ʿIshāʾ al-Ākhirah prayer.""

He added: ""And Ḥafṣah (may Allāh be pleased with her) informed me that he (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer.""

This is a ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ hadith."
435,"Abu Hurairah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Whoever prays six Rak'ah after Al-Maghrib"
436,"Abdullah bin Shaqiq said:""I asked Aishah about the Salat of Allah's Messenger (S). She said: 'He would pray four Rak'ah before Az-Zuhr and two Rak'ah after it"
"437","""I asked Aishah about the Salat of Allah's Messenger (S). She said: 'He would pray four Rak'ah before Az-Zuhr and two Rak'ah after it, and two after Al-Maghrib, and two Rak'ah after Al-Isha, and two before Al-Fajr."""
438,"Abu Hurairah narrated that:Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""The most virtuous fasting after the month of Ramadan is that of Allah's month of Al-Muharram. And the most virtuous Salat after the obligatory is the night prayer"
439,"Sa'eed bin Abi Sa'eed Al-Maqburi narrated that:Sa'eed bin Abi Sa'eed Al-Maqburi narrated that Abu Salamah informed him that he had asked Aishah: ""How was the Salat of Allah's Messenger (A) [at night] during Ramadan?"" She said: ""Allah's Messenger (S) would pray - neither in Ramadan nor in any other month - more than eleven Rak'ah. He would pray four"
"440","Sa'eed bin Abi Sa'eed Al-Maqburi narrated that Abu Salamah informed him that he had asked Aishah: ""How was the Salat of Allah's Messenger (A)  during Ramadan?"" She said: ""Allah's Messenger (S) would pray - neither in Ramadan nor in any other month - more than eleven Rak'ah. He would pray four, and do not ask about their excellence or length, then he would pray four, and do not ask about their excellence or length, then he would pray three."" Aishah said: ""I asked: 'O Messenger of Allah! Do you sleep before having performed Witr?"" He said: 'O Aishah! Indeed my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep.'"""
441,"Narrated to us al-Ḥasan ibn ‘Alī al-‘Askarī, narrated to us Abū Bakr ibn Abī al-Isḥāq, narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, narrated to us Sufyān, from Abū Isḥāq, from ‘Āmir al-Sha‘bī, from Masrūq, from ‘Ā’isha (may Allāh be pleased with her), that she said: ""Between the ‘Ishā’ prayer and the Fajr prayer, the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ‘alayhi wa sallam) used to pray eleven rak‘ahs. He would offer them in sets of two rak‘ahs, and then conclude with a single rak‘ah (Witr). When he had finished his prayer, he would lie down on his right side."""
442,"Ibn Abbas narrated:""The Prophet (S) would pray thirteen Rak'ah at night"
443,"Aishah narrated:""The Prophet (S) would pray nine Rak'ah in the night"
444,"Narrator not mentioned:(Another chain with similar narration)"
445,"Aishah narrated:""When the Prophet (S) did not pray at night because he was prevented from it by sleep or being sleepy then he would pray twelve Rak'ah during the daytime"
"446","""When the Prophet (S) did not pray at night because he was prevented from it by sleep or being sleepy then he would pray twelve Rak'ah during the daytime."""
"447","Allah's Messenger (S) said: ""Allah, Blessed and Exalted is He, descends to the earth's heaven every night when the first third of the night has passed. He says: ""I am the Sovereign. Is there any who calls upon Me so that I may respond to him? Is there any who asks of Me that I may give him? Is there any who seeks forgiveness from Me so that I may forgive him?' He continues in that until the illumination of Al-Fajr."""
"448","The Prophet (S) said to Abu Bakr: ""I passed by you while you were reciting and your voice was low."" He said: ""I let He who, I was consulting hear."" He said: ""Raise your voice."" Then he said to Umar: ""I passed by while you were reciting and your voice was loud."" So he said: ""I repel drowsiness and keep Ash-Shaitan away."" So he said: ""Lower your voice."""
449,"Abdullah bin Abi Qais narrated:""I asked Aishah how the recitation of the Prophet (S) was at night. [Would he recite silently o audibly?] So she said: 'He would do both of those. Sometimes he was silent with his recitation and sometimes it was audible.' So I said: 'All praise is due to Allah"
"450","""I asked Aishah how the recitation of the Prophet (S) was at night.  So she said: 'He would do both of those. Sometimes he was silent with his recitation and sometimes it was audible.' So I said: 'All praise is due to Allah, the One who made the matter broad.'"""
451,"Ibn Umar narrated that :The Prophet (S) said: ""Offer Salat in your homes and do not turn them into graves"
